<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maranhão</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maranhão is a state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, on the Atlantic coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> and the north-east. Its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis">São Luís</a>, was founded by the French in 1612 and taken by the Portuguese in 1615, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> occupied it from 1641 to 1644. Under Portugal it was the centre of a separate colonial state, linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> more closely than to the rest of Brazil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maranhao.jpg" alt="Maranhão" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: E2m · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_do_Maranh%C3%A3o.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1621 Philip II made it the State of Brazil and cut off a separate State of <b>Maranhão</b> at São Luís.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After a failed occupation of Salvador in 1624 and 1625, a Dutch fleet captured Olinda and Recife in Pernambuco in 1630 and gradually extended control along the coast from Sergipe to <b>Maranhão</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-brazil">Dutch Brazil</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1612 an expedition led by Daniel de La Touche, seigneur de La Ravardière, and François de Razilly, with Capuchin friars, landed on the island of <b>Maranhão</b> on Brazil's northern coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/equinoctial-france">Equinoctial France</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1751 the Marquis of Pombal reorganised the State of Maranhão as the State of Grão-Pará and <b>Maranhão</b>, moving its capital from São Luís to Belém.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-grao-para-and-maranhao">State of Grão-Pará and Maranhão</a></li><li><blockquote>After expelling the French of Equinoctial France in 1615 and founding Belém in 1616, Portugal created the State of <b>Maranhão</b> in 1621, separate from the State of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a></li><li><blockquote>When the State of Grão-Pará and Maranhão was divided in 1772, the eastern part, made up of the captaincies of <b>Maranhão</b> and Piauí, became the State of Maranhão and Piauí, with its capital at São Luís.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-maranhao-and-piaui">State of Maranhão and Piauí</a></li></ul></section>
